Record the database contents before performing backup and restoration.

## Database Persistence Verification

Exit PostgreSQL and recreate the database container:

```powershell
docker compose up -d --force-recreate database
docker exec facility-db pg_isready -U facilityuser -d facilitydb
```

After PostgreSQL is ready, verify that the original database record remains:

```powershell
docker exec facility-db psql -U facilityuser -d facilitydb -c "SELECT id, description, status FROM workorders;"
```

## Database Backup Configuration

| Parameter | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Original Volume | facility-data |
| Backup Directory | C:\FacilityBackup |
| Backup Archive | facility-data.tar.gz |
| Restored Volume | facility-restored |
| Backup Method | Physical Volume Archive |
| Recovery Method | Separate Docker Volume Restoration |

## Database Backup Procedure

### Step 1 - Stop the Database

```powershell
docker compose stop database
```

Stopping PostgreSQL before creating a physical volume backup helps prevent inconsistent database files.

### Step 2 - Create the Backup Directory

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path "C:\FacilityBackup"
```

### Step 3 - Create the Backup Archive

```powershell
docker run --rm -v facility-data:/source:ro -v "C:\FacilityBackup:/backup" alpine tar -czf /backup/facility-data.tar.gz -C /source .
```

The original database volume is mounted as read-only during the backup procedure.

The temporary backup container uses a host directory mount to store the archive. No host bind mounts are configured in the application Compose services.

### Step 4 - Verify the Backup Archive

```powershell
Get-Item "C:\FacilityBackup\facility-data.tar.gz"
```

Inspect the archive contents:

```powershell
docker run --rm -v "C:\FacilityBackup:/backup:ro" alpine tar -tzf /backup/facility-data.tar.gz
```

## Database Restoration Procedure

### Step 1 - Create a Separate Restoration Volume

```powershell
docker volume create facility-restored
```

The restoration volume must be new or empty before extracting the backup.

### Step 2 - Restore the Database Archive

```powershell
docker run --rm -v facility-restored:/restore -v "C:\FacilityBackup:/backup:ro" alpine tar -xzpf /backup/facility-data.tar.gz -C /restore
```

The backup archive is mounted as read-only during restoration.

### Step 3 - Verify the Restored Volume

```powershell
docker volume inspect facility-restored
```

### Step 4 - Update Docker Compose

Replace the original database volume configuration with:

```yaml
volumes:
  - facility-restored:/var/lib/postgresql/data
```

Declare the restored volume at the top level:

```yaml
volumes:
  facility-restored:
    name: facility-restored
    external: true
```

### Step 5 - Recreate the Database Container

```powershell
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d --force-recreate database
docker compose ps
docker exec facility-db pg_isready -U facilityuser -d facilitydb
```

### Step 6 - Verify Recovered Database Records

After PostgreSQL is ready, inspect the restored database records:

```powershell
docker exec facility-db psql -U facilityuser -d facilitydb -c "SELECT id, description, status FROM workorders;"
```

Compare the recovered database records with the original records before backup.

The original database volume is retained until successful recovery has been confirmed.

